 2022. The 2022 Budgetary Guidelines were sent to Congress for approval on April 15, 2021 and were enacted on August 20, 2021. On December 21, 2022, the 2022 budget law was approved by Congress and was enacted on January 21, 2022 (the "2022 Budget Law").\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth the assumptions that were included in the 2022 Budget Law.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table No. 3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal 2022 Budget Assumptions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                    As of December 31, 2022   \par Gross Domestic Product           \par Nominal GDP (billions of Reais)                              9,934.40   \par Real GDP Growth                                              3.10       % \par Inflation                        \par Domestic Inflation (IPCA)                                    5.75       % \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Federal Budget Secretariat (SOF) and Economic Policy Secretariat (SPE).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2023 Budget\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 2023 Budgetary Guidelines set a nominal primary deficit of R$69.01 billion (0.66% of estimated GDP) for the consolidated public sector in 2023. The macroeconomic parameters contemplated by the bill anticipate inflation at 3.30% and a projected GDP growth of 2.50% for 2023. There is no assurance that such assumptions will prevail, and it is likely that such outcomes will vary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 2023 Budgetary Guidelines were sent to Congress for approval on July 12, 2022 and were enacted on August 9, 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table No. 4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal 2023 Budget Assumptions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                    As of February 28, 2023   \par Gross Domestic Product           \par Nominal GDP (billions of Reais)                              10,711,6   \par Real GDP Growth                                              1.61       % \par Inflation                        \par Domestic Inflation (IPCA)                                    5.31       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: Federal Budget Secretariat (SOF) and Economic Policy Secretariat (SPE).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiscal Balance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In February 2023, the consolidated public sector registered a primary deficit of R$ 26.45 billion. For the 12-month period ended February 28, 2023, the accumulated balance registered a surplus of R$ 93.25 billion (0.93% of GDP).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In February 2023, the Brazilian Social Security System registered a deficit of R$ 21.05 billion, 4.70% higher (in real terms) than the deficit registered in February 2022. For the 12-month period ended February 28, 2023, the deficit of the Brazilian Social Security System totaled R$ 269.93 billion (in real terms). At current market prices, the deficit accumulated in the preceding 12-month period ended February 28, 2023, reached R$ 264.10 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PUBLIC DEBT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public Debt Indicators\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public Sector Net Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of February 28, 2023, Public Sector Net Debt was R$5,706.73 billion (56.64% of GDP) compared to R$5,047.57 billion (56.14% of GDP) as of February 28, 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Government Gross Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of February 28, 2023, General Government Gross Debt was R$7,351.86 billion (72.96% of GDP) compared to R$7,001.66 billion (77.87% of GDP) as of February 28, 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Public Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table presents Brazil's Federal Public Debt profile as of February 28, 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table No. 5\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Public Debt Profile\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                  Feb/2023                 Feb/2023             Jan/2023            Jan/2023                  Feb/2022               Feb/2022   \par                                                           %                                        %               