uring in 12 months (%)                   22.5      %                        22.6       %                        23.4      %          \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: National Treasury.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On January 23, 2023, the National Treasury released an Annual Borrowing Plan for 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table No. 6\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual Borrowing Plan 2023(1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                              Limits for 2023   \par                                              Minimum                       Maximum   \par Stock of Federal Public Debt (R$ Billion)    \par Federal Public Debt                                            6,400.0                 6,800.0   \par Composition-%                                \par Fixed Rate                                                     23.0                    27.0      \par Inflation Linked                                               29.0                    33.0      \par Floating Rate                                                  38.0                    42.0      \par Exchange Rate                                                  3.0                     7.0       \par Maturity Profile                             \par % Maturing in 12 months                                        19.0                    23.0      \par Average Maturity (years)                                       3.8                     4.2       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As published on January 23, 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Source: National Treasury.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public Debt Management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Golden Rule\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on the 2023 Budgetary Guidelines, budget execution is expect to be in compliance with the Golden Rule (as defined below). However, given the expected fiscal scenario, the Federal Government expects that it will be required to constantly monitor budget execution, with the possibility that it will need to resort to credit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an amount greater than capital expenditures, since the margins projected are only slightly positive of R$17.35 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of March 31, 2023, the budget execution is within the Golden Rule margin, in the amount of R$ 49.7 billion. For additional information on the Golden Rule, please refer to "Golden Rule" in the "Public Debt" section.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Internal Public Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Internal Public Debt Levels\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with the fiscal consolidation program promoted by the Federal Government, new BNDES Early Repayments were settled in 2022, totaling R$72.31 billion, which resulted in total payments, including regular payments, of R$83.25 billion and a total of R$1.15 billion of total repayment (including only regular payments) in 2023 as of March 15, 2023. The proceeds of BNDES Early Repayments will be used by the National Treasury exclusively for public debt relief and compliance with the Golden Rule.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regional Public Debt (State and Municipal)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Government Guarantees\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of February 28, 2023, the Federal Government had paid a total of R$53.94 billion in liabilities incurred by states and municipalities. The largest payments were attributed to (i) the State of Rio de Janeiro (R$30.99 billion), pursuant to the Special Recovery Regime (as defined below), (ii) the State of Minas Gerais (R$ 13.18 billion), (iii) the State of Goi\u225\'00\'E1s (R$4.10 billion), (iv) the State of Maranh\u227\'00\'E3o (R$1.20 billion), (v) the State of Rio Grande do Sul (R$1.00 billion), and (vi) the State of Pernambuco (R$866.27 million). The Federal Government is currently prevented by the Federal Supreme Court (Supremo Tribunal Federal or the "STF") from recovering for any guarantees granted pursuant to the Special Recovery Regime. For more information on Federal Government guarantees, please refer to "Special Recovery Regime" in the "Public Debt" section.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rating Agencies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In December 2022, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2  confirmed Brazil's long term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit rating at BB-, and revised the outlook to stable from negative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/205317/0001193125-23-100696-index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 This content was originally posted here}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Disclaimer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Government of Brazil published this content on 13 April 2023 and is solely responsible for the information contained therein. Distributed by \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.publicnow.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public}}}\uc2 \f2 , unedited and unaltered, on 13 April 2023 21:29:58 UTC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument SAEXC00020230413ej4d00gau\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
