\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Left manages better economy and Lula is right about interest rates, says Nobel\b0\par\par\uc2 2,867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The center-left governments have become better managers of the economy than the right in the 21st century, argues \uc2 Joseph Stiglitz\uc2 , winner of the Nobel Prize in economics, professor at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  (USA) and former chief economist of the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  (1997-2000).\par \par \uc2 For Stiglitz, who turned 80 this February, the center-left is returning to power in Latin America at a moment "that couldn't be worse", with pandemics, inflation, fiscal restrictions and a slowing world economy.\par \par \uc2 But he believes that the region's governments can succeed if they manage to keep their focus on the fact that they were elected to create a "shared prosperity", that is, an inclusive growth that ensures the improvement of the lives of the poorest part of the population.\par \par \uc2 In an exclusive interview with BBC News Brazil, Stiglitz affirms that the world's central banks are wrong to fight the current inflation by raising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 This is because, in the economist's assessment, the high cost of living that afflicts the world today is mainly caused by restrictions on the supply side caused by the pandemic and the Ukraine War, in addition to changes in consumption patterns also derived from the health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Thus, in the current context, raising interest rates -a monetary policy measure that aims to restrict the cost and supply of credit, cooling the economy to reduce inflation- may do more harm than good, argues Nobel.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, in the dispute between President \uc2 Luiz In\u225\'00\'E1cio Lula da Silva\uc2  (PT) and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Roberto Campos Neto\uc2  over the level of the basic interest rate and the country's inflation target, Stiglitz assesses that the PT president is correct in his concerns.\par \par \uc2 "There is an enormous cost in having high interest rates. This puts Brazil at a competitive disadvantage, strangles Brazilian companies, weakens the country's economy. So President Lula is absolutely correct to be concerned about these issues," Stiglitz tells BBC News Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "The most recent theoretical research over a long period of time shows that in times of rapid adjustment of the economy and structural change -the kind of thing we are experiencing in the post-covid world and as we head towards the green transition-, a higher inflation rate actually facilitates adjustment," he says.\par \par \uc2 Advisor during the administration of democrat \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  (1995-1997) and currently co-chairman of ICRICT (Independent Commission on International Corporate Tax Reform), Stiglitz defends that the fight against inequality should be at the top of the Brazilian tax reform priorities - whose proposal the Lula government intends to present still in this first semester.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously it is important to have an efficient tax system, and this requires simplification. But what is even more or equally important for Brazil is to reformulate the tax system to fight inequality," says the economist, one of the defenders of a global tax on multinationals and increasing taxation on the richest.\par \par \uc2 "I cannot opine on Brazilian politics, but I believe that increasing the progressiveness of Brazil's tax system should be a priority. Given the high level of inequality in the country, this should be at the top of the agenda."\par \par \uc2 Check out the main excerpts of the interview below.\par \par \uc2 In a recent article, you argued that raising interest rates is not the best way to fight inflation when it is mainly caused by supply constraints and changes in the consumption pattern. Do you believe that the world's central banks are wrong in the way they are combating the current global rise in the cost of living?\par \par \uc2 First, let me say that interest rates were abnormally low, close to zero since the great recession of 2008. So it made sense for central banks to take advantage of the current situation to normalize interest rates.\par \par \uc2 But now we are past this point, beyond normalization. And I believe this is a mistake.\par \par \uc2 Why do you believe this?\par \par \uc2 When I wrote that article, the evidence was clear to me that the main source of inflation was supply-side disruptions caused by the pandemic and to some extent by the Russian invasion of Ukraine. There were also some pandemic-related demand-side shocks that were also inflationary.\par \par \uc2 Since I wrote that, the evidence has reinforced my conclusion, with [U.S.] inflation receding further as supply-side bottlenecks have been resolved, oil prices have come down, and other prices have normalized.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that central banks worry about, and rightly so, are price and wage spirals [when inflation drives wage increases, which further fuels inflation]. And there is no evidence of that, wages [in the US] are not keeping up with prices, real income is falling, and inflation expectations remain weak, showing that market participants seem to have views consistent with what I have presented.\par \par \uc2 All of this means that the policy of raising interest rates, which is the normal response to excess aggregate demand, is inappropriate in the current context. And one of the things that I argue is that it may actually exacerbate inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Because, for example, one of the things that is needed to relieve supply-side pressures is investment. And high interest rates make that investment more difficult.\par \par \uc2 And do you think this is valid only for the US or can the same argument be used for other countries facing inflation at the moment?\par \par \uc2 This argument applies to almost all countries that are facing inflation at the moment. In most of them, the argument is even stronger, because in most of them much of the inflation is imported. That is, it comes from products brought or priced abroad.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, President Lula has been fighting for weeks with the president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Roberto Campos Neto\uc2 , about the level of the country's basic interest rate, which is currently above 13%. Lula argues that interest rates are strangling the economy, while \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  defends the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mandate to pursue the country's inflation target of around 3%. How do you see this dispute in Brazil?\par \par \uc2 First, you have to say that inflation targets -which in Europe [and in the U.S.] is 2%, and you talked about 3% [in Brazil]- are made out of nothing. They have no basis in economic theory or in economic experience.\par \par \uc2 There is concern about an inflationary spiral, but right now there is no evidence of that. I don't know all the details for Brazil, but I can say emphatically that there is no evidence of an inflationary spiral in the US and generally globally.\par \par \uc2 So it is not a magic number, 2% or 3%, but if there is an inflationary spiral that is getting out of control.\par \par \uc2 There is a huge cost in having high interest rates. It puts Brazil at a competitive disadvantage, it strangles Brazilian companies, it weakens the economy of the country. So President Lula is absolutely right to be concerned about these issues.\par \par \uc2 Going back to the inflation targeting issue, the most recent theoretical research, conducted over a long period of time, shows that in times of rapid adjustment of the economy and structural change - the kind of thing we are experiencing in the post-covid world and as we head towards the green transition - a higher inflation rate actually facilitates the adjustment.\par \par \uc2 So economic performance in general will be better if the inflation rate is slightly higher. I believe this emphatically in the case of the U.S., that we should not limit ourselves to [an inflation target of] 2%.\par \par \uc2 Finally, even if you believe that there should be a 2% or 3% target, there is no theory or empirical evidence that going back [from higher inflation] to that 2% or 3% in a short period of time is best practice. Just as the number is taken out of thin air, the speed to get back to that number is taken out of thin air.\par \par \uc2 While this debate is going on in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many economists have recalled unsuccessful \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in monetary policy by governments in recent years. They cite cases such as Turkey and Argentina, which ended up resulting in more inflation and a sharp devaluation of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . How do you see these concerns? Do you believe that this could be a risk for Brazil, should Lula succeed in changing the law that currently guarantees the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I think there are separate issues. Obviously some governments have weak institutions and inherited institutional problems that go beyond just one aspect, such as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So it would be wrong to say that because Zimbabwe or Venezuela has a problem, our hands should be tied.\par \par \uc2 Speaking from the US perspective, we have a strong democracy -or at least we did before Trump- and our political leaders know that they can be held accountable and that if there is an inflationary spiral, they will pay the price.\par \par \uc2 So it's in their interest to keep inflation under control, reduce it, and at the same time protect workers and those who are negatively affected by inflation. So you have to do both [control inflation and ensure job creation].\par \par \uc2 More broadly, when you face complex social and economic changes, you need coordination between fiscal and monetary policies [fiscal policy is about controlling government spending and revenue collection, while monetary policy is about controlling the amount of money in circulation in the economy, which is done through the interest rate].\par \par \uc2 In the US we have an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Paul Volcker, a prominent chairman of the board of the Federal Reserve [Fed, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], once said, "Congress created us, and Congress can 'uncreate' us."\par \par \uc2 So he was very aware that his independence was not absolute, and that he needed to act in certain ways that responded to the needs of society. This means that he needed in a certain sense to coordinate his action with what was going on.\par \par \uc2 So the question of the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sometimes taken as something sacred. In my view, it is good structurally, institutionally, but it needs to recognize the need for coordination, and also for expertise and professionalism.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to another area of your expertise, Lula's economic team hopes to approve a tax reform this year. This reform should have a first stage focused on simplifying consumption taxes into a value-added tax, and a second stage focused on income tax. As someone who has been discussing for years the use of taxation as a way to fight inequality, what is your advice for Brazil, on the eve of a reform?\par \par \uc2 Obviously it is important to have an efficient tax system, and this requires simplification. But what is even more or equally important for Brazil is to reform the tax system to fight inequality, making this system more progressive [that collects more from those with more income and wealth].\par \par \uc2 I cannot give an opinion about Brazilian politics, but I believe that increasing the progressiveness of Brazil's tax system should be a priority. Given the high level of inequality in the country, this should be at the top of the agenda.\par \par \uc2 And what would be the ways to do this? Changing income taxation or taxing the richest, those could be some of the ways to achieve this?\par \par \uc2 Income tax, wealth tax, estate tax, and raising corporate income tax are effective tools to tackle inequality, but they are not the only ones. Several Latin American countries are discussing how to increase the degree of progressiveness of their tax systems, Chile and Colombia in particular. And, given the high level of inequality in Brazil, I believe this should be a priority.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to the relationship between Brazil and the United States. The US has indicated that it should donate to the Brazilian Amazon Fund, after the meeting between Biden and Lula in early February. The amount of the donation has not been defined yet, but the initial figure of US$ 50 million would have disappointed the Brazilian authorities. Do you believe that the Biden administration is doing enough to help developing countries fight climate change?\par \par \uc2 In short, no. But the room for maneuver is limited because the Democrats don't have enough control over Congress to guarantee the necessary budget. We've had a tough battle to get funding for green investments in the U.S. that should not only accelerate our green transition, but also increase productivity in the country.\par \par \uc2 So I believe that we should be doing more, that climate change is a global issue, and since we are the richest country in the world, we should make a commensurate effort.\par \par \uc2 But I am hopeful that with the choice of a new \uc2 World Bank\uc2  president, which should be announced soon, the bank can make a greater effort, with the support of the US, to address the issue of climate change in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 [BBC News Brazil Editor's Note: Current \uc2 World Bank\uc2  President \uc2 David Malpass\uc2  announced on February 15 that he will step down in June, a year before the end of his term. The unexpected resignation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's nominee comes amid criticism of the bank's timid performance on issues such as fighting pandemic disease, poverty and global warming. Last week, Biden appointed Indian-American businessman \uc2 Ajay Banga\uc2 , former CEO of \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , to the post. The appointment will still have to be confirmed by the bank's board of directors].\par \par \uc2 At a time when Brazil is trying to reaffirm its place in the world as a leader in sustainability and the government is seeking to recover the economy after years of low growth, what can be the role of green investments in this process? Do you believe that Brazil should seek its own "Green New Deal" [a proposal from the left wing of the U.S. Democratic Party that associates an ecological agenda with job creation]?\par \par \uc2 Yes, I believe that all countries need to recognize that we are moving quickly from fossil fuels to green energy.\par \par \uc2 The countries that move sooner and faster will have a competitive advantage. They will learn to master the new technologies. In economics we talk about "moving up the knowledge curve.\par \par \uc2 Given Brazil's high level of technical competence, the quality of its engineers, and the diversity of its economy, I believe that Brazil is in a position to play a leading role among the emerging countries in this transition to a green economy.\par \par \uc2 In a BBC interview in 2020you said that the wave of protests in Latin America at that time was happening too late, given the level of inequality in the region. As a result of that wave of discontent, many countries elected left-wing governments. But there is a fear that these new governments will have short "honeymoons", given the discontent that is still present, combined with high inflation and fiscal restrictions. Do you think it is possible for these governments to succeed in this context and in the midst of a slowing world economy?\par \par \uc2 Yes, but it will be difficult. They came to power at a very, very complicated time. There is the pandemic, the inflation.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Brazil, Lula inherited an absolute mess from the Bolsonaro government. To some extent, you could say that it couldn't be worse, because he starts in an environment where it is necessary to fix the chaos created by the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, for example, the Republicans are always talking about fiscal responsibility, but every time they get into government, they create huge deficits. Trump did that, [Ronald] Reagan did that. So every time the Democrats need to fix the inherited mess. It took [Bill] Clinton to rebalance the Budget [after George H.W. Bush's term].\par \par \uc2 So there is always a disadvantage for responsible center-left governments, like Lula, to fix the inherited mess. And the chaos is even greater because Bolsonaro, like Trump, has divided society. And, obviously, when you have a polarized society it is much harder to achieve the solidarity and coherence that would help address social problems.\par \par \uc2 I believe the answer has to be, as we say in the US, that these center-left governments can't take their eye off the ball. They were elected to create a better sense of shared prosperity [another way of saying inclusive growth, which includes improving the lives of the poorest part of the population].\par \par \uc2 It is interesting that in many ways they [the center-left governments] have become better managers of the economy. I say this because the economy of the 21st century is based on innovation, competition, a high level of human capital, and good public infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And the right-wing governments they replaced were centered on monopolies, big business, limited competition, and insufficient investment in people and infrastructure. The right-wing economic agenda led to low growth and poor economic performance.\par \par \uc2 So while center-left governments have inherited a mess, if they keep their eye on the ball and focus on the goal of attacking these problems and creating shared prosperity, I believe they will succeed.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230228ej2s005ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
