\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Latin American markets fall on low volumes; stock markets gain amid lower risk aversion\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Froilan Romero SANTIAGO, Dec 27 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded with losses on Tuesday, while the bulk of stock markets posted gains, amid reduced risk aversion, after China announced it will remove its COVID-19 quarantine rule for inbound travelers, an important step in reopening its borders. * China will stop requiring quarantine for incoming travelers from Jan. 8, the National Health Commission said Monday, eliminating a rule in place since the start of the pandemic three years ago. * China's handling of COVID-19 will also be downgraded to category B, less stringent than the current top-level A, the health authority said in a statement, as the disease has become less virulent and gradually evolves into a common respiratory infection. * The measures announced by China also limited the dollar's rise in global markets, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted a gain of around 0.2% against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that make up the dollar index.\par \par \uc2  * The Mexican peso was trading at 19.4315 per dollar, with a loss of 0.26% against the Reuters reference price on Monday, in line with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, in a volatile market due to a reduced business volume that watched news on the easing of restrictions by COVID-19 in China for incoming travelers to its territory. * "For the moment, the range between 19.30 and 19.45 continues to be respected, something that should not change given the low volume traded," said Monex Grupo Financiero in an analysis note. * The main stock index S&P;/BMV IPC, which comprises the 35 most liquid companies in the Mexican market, rose 0.37% to 50,457.96 units, encouraged by a global appetite for risk after China's announcement. * "The easing of the zero COVID-19 policy in China favors economic growth, not only in China but in the world," said local firm Banco Base in an analysis note. "Moreover, it reduces the risk of supply chain disruptions," it added. * However, the Brazilian real depreciated 1.18% to 5.2794 units per dollar, while the Bovespa index of the B3 Sao Paulo stock exchange gave up 1.01% to 107,642.68 points. * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, selling 2 billion dollars in the spot market through a repurchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the agency announced in a statement published on its website. * The markets in Brazil reflected investors' caution regarding the guidelines to be adopted by the new government and awaiting the nomination of new ministers to accompany President-elect \uc2 Luiz In\u225\'00\'E1cio Lula da Silva\uc2 . * In Argentina, the peso fell 0.19% to 176.10 per dollar in a depreciation regulated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Merval stock index rose 0.25% to 192,957.11 units, due to renewed purchases encouraged by competitive prices in pesos and as a hedge against the devaluation of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said traders. * This market slightly exceeds an annual increase of 120%, with gains of around 30% in dollar conversion, to place it among the most profitable in the global context. * Meanwhile, the Argentine peso in the influential informal market fell on Tuesday to its historic low of 352 units per dollar, in the face of a persistent demand for hedging positions before the end of the year and the absence of genuine sellers, said traders. * The Chilean peso appreciated 0.81% to 875.50/875.80 units per dollar. Meanwhile, the leading index of the Santiago Stock Exchange, the IPSA, advanced 0.32% to 5,237.00 points. * The Colombian peso weakened 0.49% to 4,760 units per dollar in an anemic market; while in the stock market, the MSCI COLCAP stock index rose 0.31% to 1,251.40 points. * The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, fell just 0.03% to 3.817/3.819 units per dollar, while the Lima Stock Exchange benchmark improved 0.39% to 557.50 points. Quotations at 1459 GMT Indices Quotation Var pct Var pct daily stocks in the year MSCI Emerging Markets 956.48 -22.46 0.18 MSCI Latin America 2,176.06 3.9 -0.83 Bovespa Brazil 108,155.18 3.1794 -0.54\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221228eicr000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
