\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The Economist: emerging markets look unusually resilient\b0\par\par\uc2 1,072 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The script is familiar. A Federal Reserve bent on controlling inflation ruthlessly raises rates. The dollar soars, global financial conditions tighten and the world economy falls into a generalized slowdown. But this time, there is a twist. Where normally one would write about an emerging market crisis, instead there is an eerie calm.\par \par \uc2 For decades, fast-growing middle-income countries have been a source of financial trouble. In the early 1980s, the Fed's crusade against double-digit inflation triggered a debt crisis in Latin America; in the 2010s, policy normalization after the global financial crisis shook the "fragile five" (Brazil, India, Indonesia, South Africa and Turkey). The same could have been expected during the current tightening, which is the most intense since the early 1980s.\par \par \uc2 In forecasts released on October 11, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  again downgraded its global growth projections and warned that economies accounting for a third of world GDP are heading for recession. The world's poorest countries are on the ropes. More than a billion people live in economies now facing severe hardship.\par \par \uc2 And yet most large middle-income countries are weathering the storm. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  reckons that emerging economies will substantially outperform the rich ones this year and next, despite the slowdown in China and contraction in Russia. While the euro, pound and yen are sliding against the dollar, the Indian rupiah and Indonesian rupiah have managed a more elegant fall, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Mexico have risen. Emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking place in London rather than Brasilia.\par \par \uc2 The resilience of the emerging world is partly a story of maturation. Since the crises of the 1980s and 1990s, local financial markets have deepened and banks are better managed. Policymaking has improved. Officials have learned the dangers of sloppy budgeting and large current account deficits. Central banks are more independent and have adopted inflation targeting approaches used in the rich world.\par \par \uc2 This sophistication and care has proven its worth in the last two years. Many middle-income central banks started raising rates much earlier than rich countries. This prevented rising inflation from getting out of control and also stopped destabilizing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines. Such is the case in Brazil, which experienced hyperinflation as recently as the early 1990s, but has worked in recent decades to establish the credibility and independence of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When inflation spiked and the real faltered early last year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded with aggressive rate hikes, amounting to a cumulative increase of nearly 12 percentage points. Inflation has fallen from a peak of 12% in April to less than 8%; the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been among the world's best performers. Meanwhile, in the rich world, central banks that have lagged the Fed's tightening program, such as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , have experienced dizzying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations and have yet to reach peak inflation.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market exchange rate regimes have also improved. These economies once relied on fixed exchange rates to contain inflation and ensure cheaper credit. But the crisis years encouraged a move in the direction of floating rate regimes, in which markets have more say over the value of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now, most governments \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only occasionally to support undesirably fast or large movements.\par \par \uc2 Many have associated this with larger \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. During good times, they bought assets denominated in reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as dollars. This slows the pace of appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and creates a safe asset pile. In 1998, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves amounted to 5% of world GDP. By 2020, that figure had risen to 15%, representing a staggering $13 trillion. While China's reserves of more than $3 trillion represent a large share, other emerging market governments have accumulated formidable piles. For example, India's total exceeds $500 billion, and Brazil's is worth more than $300 billion.\par \par \uc2 These reserves can be deployed to slow the depreciation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when investors' appetite for risk falls. This year, India sold US$ 40 billion to keep the rupee's decline modest and orderly. However, reserves are most valuable in the midst of a crisis, when they can be used to pay for crucial imports and meet hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt payments. Fundamentally, they help reassure foreign investors that obligations will be met.\par \par \uc2 And emerging economies have addressed their biggest weakness: the inability to borrow in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Governments once had no choice but to accept loans denominated in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This vulnerability, called "original sin," could turn a drop in investor sentiment into a financial catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 Because a fall in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased the burden of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, economic weakness or nervous markets could set in motion a cycle of capital flight, increased pressure to devalue and loss of confidence in government solvency, often ending in a chaotic default.\par \par \uc2 But after the global financial crisis, bond yields in the rich world plummeted, pushing investors to hunt for yield elsewhere. This hunt, combined with better economic management in emerging markets, allowed officials to shift borrowing to local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. In the mid-2000s, about 46% of Indonesia's public debt and 83% of Chile's debt was owed in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By 2021, those figures had fallen to 23% and 32%.\par \par \uc2 The security gained by these innovations is impressive. But in a prohibitive economic climate, emerging markets cannot afford to take a victory lap. While governments have borrowed more in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many companies have not, and if global problems force large companies to seek bailouts, their external obligations could become external obligations of their governments. If worsening financial conditions trigger a flight to safety, a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  focused squarely on high U.S. inflation may not come to the world's rescue with a torrent of emergency lending, as in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Stability may also lead to greater risk-taking. The healthier financial position of emerging markets has allowed some to take on debt that once would have seemed too high even for rich countries. India's debt has risen to 84% of GDP; Brazil's stands at 88%. In the early 2000s, American and European eminences convinced themselves, to their later regret, that financial crises were something that only affected poorer countries. Looking back at recent history, the correct conclusion to draw is not that emerging markets are safe. It is that they are nowhere.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221019eiaj00697\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
