\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Products whose prices have skyrocketed in Latin America\b0\par\par\uc2 1,072 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Grocery shopping or filling up the car with gas has become increasingly difficult for millions of Latin Americans as the cost of living rises daily.\par \par \uc2 The common complaint that "the salary is not enough" is now at a critical point given the high levels of year-on-year inflation in the region.\par \par \uc2 "Latin America will be the region with the highest inflation on the planet this year," Juan Carlos Martinez, professor of economics at \uc2 IE Business School\uc2 , Spain, tells BBC Mundo.\par \par \uc2 "What we see is a perfect storm," he says, because there is a global context of high liquidity, bottlenecks in supply chains, rising commodity prices (especially food and energy), depreciation of Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a strong recovery in consumption.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at what is happening in the largest economies in the region: the cost of living in Argentina has skyrocketed to 52.1%, while in Brazil it rose 11.1% and in Mexico 6.2% in October, compared to the same month last year.\par \par \uc2 They are followed by Chile with 6%, Peru with 5.8% and Colombia with 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Like a plane that took off suddenly after a recessionary 2020 hit by the COVID-19 pandemic, inflation is not only spilling over in Latin America, but also in countries like the United States where it reached 6.2% in October, the highest figure recorded in the country in the last 30 years.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation is a danger in the region," said Benjamin Gedan, deputy director of the Latin America Program at the Washington-based Wilson Center, in an interview with BBC Mundo.\par \par \uc2 "Latin America is looking at a future of high inflation and low growth, a bleak prognosis for its post-pandemic prospects," he adds.\par \par \uc2 The "most expensive" products\par \par \uc2 Clearly the prices of food, gasoline, gas and electricity are the ones leading the inflationary rises.\par \par \uc2 Being basic necessities, it is the most vulnerable families who suffer the harshest effects of the increase in the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 Comparing October of this year with the same month last year, in Mexico cooking oil jumped 32%, while avocado and LP domestic gas are also skyrocketing (although in the case of gas, the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by setting a maximum limit).\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , inflation reached its highest level in 19 years. Gasoline, electric light tariffs, food and clothing have led the price increases, a big stone in the shoe for President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Jair Bolsonaro's government is trying to get around strict spending limits to increase his chances of re-election in 2022," Robert Wood, chief economist for Latin America at the Economist Intelligence Unit, tells BBC Mundo.\par \par \uc2 The country has had the worst drought in nearly a century, with a drop in hydroelectric generation and, therefore, an increase in electricity rates. On top of that, a sharp drop in the exchange rate has contributed to the inflationary crisis.\par \par \uc2 Leaving aside Venezuela's hyperinflation, Argentina's is by far the highest in the region. Latin America's third-largest economy has almost five times more inflation than Brazil and more than eight times that of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Having costs increase 1% every week, on average, pulverizes Argentines' incomes.\par \par \uc2 The products that rose the most in October were food and non-alcoholic beverages, while so far this year the prices of clothing and footwear, along with health costs, have pushed inflation up sharply.\par \par \uc2 With the aim of slowing the rise in food prices, the government of Peronist Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez ordered a price freeze in early October on more than 1,400 products in the so-called "basic food basket" until Jan. 7.\par \par \uc2 "Argentina has adopted an unsuccessful approach, which has been based on limits on beef exports, heavily subsidized energy and a wide range of price controls," says Gedan.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, inflation has reached its highest level in 13 years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to raise interest rates to counter rising prices.\par \par \uc2 The products that have risen the most in the last year are: air travel (79.5%), package tours, liquefied gas and gasoline.\par \par \uc2 To these are added used cars and some foodstuffs.\par \par \uc2 The basic family basket in Peru increased by 5.8% in October, driven by the increase in the value of raw materials worldwide, among other reasons.\par \par \uc2 This happens because the country imports most of the corn, wheat, diesel or soybean oil it consumes.\par \par \uc2 Imported products are used to produce food such as chicken, eggs, bread, noodles and oil, as well as fuel for domestic use and transportation.\par \par \uc2 That explains the rising cost of living in the South American country.\par \par \uc2 Internationally, the price of oil so far this year has risen 69%, the price of wheat since the end of 2019 has risen almost 69% and soybean oil has gone up 100%\par \par \uc2 Food and non-alcoholic beverages, accommodation, water, electricity, gas and other fuels are some of the products that in October have experienced the highest rises in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 In relation to food, the prices of products such as meat, fruit and milk continue to rise, as well as potatoes, edible oils and tomatoes.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela, with a chronic annualized hyperinflation of almost 2,000%, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Venezuela\uc2  (\uc2 BCV\uc2 ), and with a projection of 2,700% by 2021 according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ), has been leading the cost of living increase in Latin America and the world for years.\par \par \uc2 What are the countries of the region doing?\par \par \uc2 Nikhil Sanghani, an economist specializing in Latin America at the British consulting firm \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , explained to BBC Mundo that the largest economies in the region are trying to control the escalation of prices by raising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the countries, such as Brazil and Chile, have done so quite aggressively," he says.\par \par \uc2 The wave of tighter monetary policy "looks to be a major headwind for economic recovery next year," says Robert Wood.\par \par \uc2 That's because sharp rises in the cost of money will have a slowing effect on the economy, which is why it's crucial how each \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  finds a way to balance the challenges ahead.\par \par \uc2 In that context, he projects, "the average regional growth rate will be 2.5% in 2022.\par \par \uc2 An estimate that may vary as new signs appear on the horizon, but what is clear is that next year will come with a lot of difficulties on the way.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211119ehbj002pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
