\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Markets Plummet:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sold $500 Million to Support the Real\b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday made its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March, pushing the real away from near six-month lows amid fresh concerns about looser government spending ahead of elections next year.\par \par \uc2 The government is preparing an expanded welfare program next year, a person with knowledge of the plans told Reuters, with part of the plan accounted for outside a constitutional spending limit, which investors have used as a proxy for fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 O Estado de S.Paulo newspaper reported that the social assistance program could cost around 90 billion reais (about $16 billion), with a third of that counted outside the spending limit. An O Globo columnist reported that 50 billion reais would be counted outside the limit.\par \par \uc2 The reports stoked fears that far-right President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  would loosen fiscal controls and increase spending to regain his waning popularity ahead of a re-election bid. The Economy Ministry did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Against this backdrop, the benchmark Bovespa stock market index fell nearly 3.5% and the real lost 1.3% to nearly 5.60 to the dollar, testing a six-month low, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a further drop in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $500 million in a spot auction after a week of increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap sales that have failed to shore up the real, which is down about 7% against the dollar so far this year. Investors in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been spooked by political risks, a fragile economic recovery and the prospect of rising interest rates in richer economies.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's own \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already raised interest rates by 4.25 percentage points this year to combat inflation soaring into double digits and signaled 100 basis point increases at upcoming meetings, while emphasizing the need for continued fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 But the government faces mounting political pressure to spend more as the economic recovery slows, unemployment remains near 14% and poverty soars.\par \par \uc2 In an interview published Monday night by news magazine Veja, Arthur Lira, speaker of the lower house of Congress, defended new social spending outside the spending limit . Lira assured Veja that the terrible impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in Brazil, which has the second highest death toll from the virus after the United States, meant that fiscal responsibility could not be prioritized over the urgent needs of the most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211019ehaj009a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
