\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Latin American stock markets and  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  remain uneven, Brazil's markets fall on fiscal uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Nelson Bocanegra\par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Latin American stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were mixed at mid-session on Tuesday, amid global dollar weakness and increased appetite for risky assets spurred by positive U.S. corporate results.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's assets led losses in reaction to news about the debates in Brasilia over higher spending on social benefits. The real extended the decline to 0.89% and the Bovespa stock index plunged 2.36%.\par \par \uc2 * The newspaper O Estado de Sao Paulo reported that the social assistance program could cost around 90 billion reais (about $16 billion), and a third of that was counted outside the spending limit. An O Globo columnist reported that 50 billion reais would be counted outside the limit.\par \par \uc2 The fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March.\par \par \uc2 * "Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  start the day mixed but with a positive bias. However, the real is the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on account of the announcement of social programs that would take some of the spending over the limits of law," said Andres Cuartas, international fixed income manager at Renta4Global.\par \par \uc2 "In general, traders continue to expect that the season of delivery of results of U.S. companies remain positive and support a more proriesgo dynamic," he added.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a three-week low, hit by a proliferation of interest rate hike bets in other markets.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso led gains in the region by marking a 1.06% advance at the close, at 810.50/810.80 units per dollar, while the Santiago stock exchange's main index, the IPSA, fell 0.03% to 4,007.65 points.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso followed, which appreciated 0.88%, while the benchmark S&P/BMV IPC stock index lost 0.49% as the market awaited corporate reports for the third quarter, such as that of telecommunications giant \uc2 America Movil\uc2 , after the close of the session.\par \par \uc2 The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, gained 0.08% to 3.944/3.946 units per dollar. Meanwhile, the Lima Stock Exchange benchmark rose 0.88% to 546.92 points.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the Colombian peso fell 0.17% to 3,769.98 units per dollar and the benchmark index of the stock market, the MSCI COLCAP, maintained a 0.45% drop to 1,419.82 points.\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, the peso was down 0.05%, while in the stock market the Merval index rose 0.27% in its seventh session up due to selective hedging purchases by the devaluation of the peso and high inflation that hit the local economy, amid doubts of local investors before the legislative elections in November.\par \par \uc2 Quotes at 1645 GMT\par \par \uc2 Indices Share price Var pct Var pct\par \par \uc2 daily stock indexes in the year\par \par \uc2 MSCI Emerging Markets\par \par \uc2 1.283,02 -0,68\par \par \uc2 -0,05\par \par \uc2 MSCI Latin America\par \par \uc2 2.273,49 -1 -7,27\par \par \uc2 Bovespa Brazil\par \par \uc2 111.508,34 -6,3091\par \par \uc2 -2,55\par \par \uc2 IPC Mexico\par \par \uc2 52.387,74 18,88\par \par \uc2 -0,57\par \par \uc2 Argentina MerVal\par \par \uc2 83.541,93 175,62\par \par \uc2 0,823\par \par \uc2 COLCAP Colombia\par \par \uc2 1.418,29 -1,37\par \par \uc2 -0,55\par \par \uc2 IPSA Chile\par \par \uc2 4.007,57 -21,50\par \par \uc2 -0,03\par \par \uc2 Selective Peru\par \par \uc2 546,94 6,75\par \par \uc2 0,89\par \par \uc2 Dollar vs. exchange rate Var pct Var pct\par \par \uc2 to monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the year\par \par \uc2 Brazilian real\par \par \uc2 5,5586 -6,56\par \par \uc2 -2,07\par \par \uc2 Mexican peso\par \par \uc2 20,2368 -1,69\par \par \uc2 2,00\par \par \uc2 Chilean peso\par \par \uc2 811,0 -12,38\par \par \uc2 -0,08\par \par \uc2 Colombian Peso\par \par \uc2 3.764,98 -9,02\par \par \uc2 1,11\par \par \uc2 Peruvian sol\par \par \uc2 3,9362 -8,40\par \par \uc2 4,94\par \par \uc2 Argentine peso\par \par \uc2 99,30 -15,39\par \par \uc2 -0,57\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nelson Bocanegra, additional reporting by Froil\u225\'00\'E1n Romero and Benjamin Mejias in Santiago. Edited by Javier Leira)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211019ehaj007ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
