ht22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are denominated in or indexed to Brazilian reais. We also have assets and liabilities denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mainly U.S. dollars, as well as assets and liabilities that, although denominated in Brazilian reais, are indexed to U.S. dollars and, therefore, expose us to exchange rate risk. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil regulates our foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions. Please see section '\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk' of the Complete Financial Statements (IFRS), Note 32 - Risk and capital management for further information. The gap management policy adopted by the CSRML takes into account tax effects related to our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions. Since gains from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate variation on foreign investments are given a specific treatment, we have set up hedge (a liability in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative instruments) in amount sufficient so that our total \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, net of tax effects, is consistent with our low risk exposure strategy. Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  position on the liability side is composed of various elements, including the issuance of securities in international capital markets, credit from foreign banks used to finance import and export transactions, dollar-linked onlending from government financial institutions and deposits in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Latin America countries. The proceeds of these financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are usually invested in loans and purchase of dollar-linked securities. The information below has been prepared on a consolidated basis, eliminating related-party transactions. Foreign in