\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  rise as U.S. inflation data dents dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2021\par 21:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Mexican peso hits two-month high\par     * Russian rouble jump ~2%\par     * Argentina's inflation expected to heat up again in March\par     * GPA SA rises on potential IPO related to e-comm unit \par \par  (Updates prices)\par     By Susan Mathew\par     April 13 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rallied\par on Tuesday against a dollar weakened by U.S. inflation data\par signaling the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would stay easy, while\par Russia's rouble jumped after reports of a call between\par Washington and Moscow. \par     Brazil's real firmed 0.5%, while Mexico's peso\par hit a two-month high. Higher copper prices bolstered top\par producer Chile's peso, which was up 0.7% after two\par straight days of losses.\par     The rouble's recovery gained pace, up almost 2% in\par late trade, after reports that U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joe Biden\f28 \uc2 \f2  had a\par phone conversation with Russian President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as the\par market kept a close watch on geopolitical tensions between\par Moscow and the West.\par     The dollar fell after data showed U.S. consumer prices\par increased by the most in more than 8-1/2 years in March and\par underlying inflation picked up. \par     But market experts noted that the Fed's stance on inflation\par had helped the market price in the data.\par \par     "It wasn't a blowout number by any stretch, it is higher\par than the expectation by a tenth of a percent, but (Fed Chair\par Jerome) Powell did a very good job of jawboning," said Ken\par Polcari, managing partner at Kace Capital Advisors in Jupiter,\par Florida.\par     Meanwhile, \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  recommended selling emerging market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday, citing rising coronavirus cases and\par slower vaccination rollouts in developing economies as one of\par the reasons.\par     Brazil added an additional 1,480 people to its COVID-19\par death toll on Monday, as a looming investigation of how the\par government has handled the outbreak puts increased pressure on\par right-wing President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jair Bolsonaro\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     In Peru, the sol \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell about 0.3% a day after\par a socialist surge in presidential elections roiled markets.\par \par     Socialist Pedro Castillo and conservative \f28 \uc2 \f2 Keiko Fujimori\f28 \uc2 \f2  are\par projected to go into a run-off vote. \par     A Reuters poll showed that Argentina's stubbornly high\par inflation is expected to heat up again in March, advancing 4% in\par the month, driven mainly by food and education costs. The\par country is expected to see 46% inflation this year, a recent\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  poll showed. \par     The Argentine peso held steady, helped by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Among stocks, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Bovespa erased losses to\par trade higher led by consumer and healthcare stocks. Food\par retailer GPA SA rose 4.3% after the announcement of a\par potential IPO related to its e-commerce unit CNOVA.\par     Colombian stocks extended losses to a fourth day, while\par Chile's IPSA index posted its worst day in three\par weeks.\par         \par     Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1900 GMT:\par   Stock indexes           