\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real slumps to three-month low as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  selling $2 bln\b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 21:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with closing prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jamie McGeever\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, March 2 (Reuters) - Brazil's real slid to a three-month low on Tuesday as investor concern over the government's fiscal health deepened, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's twice \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market selling more than $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 A $1 billion sale was followed up with a sale of $1.095 bln after the real had slumped to a new three-month low of 5.73 per dollar, taking \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Thursday last week to over $5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The real recovered some ground but still ended the session at 5.6662 per dollar, its weakest close since Nov. 3 last year.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian assets were under heavy pressure on Tuesday after President Jair Bolsonaro eliminated certain fuel taxes and increased a tax on banks, intensifying concern about the fiscal trajectory and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's predicament with interest rates at record lows but inflation rising.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to tame volatility and deal with liquidity issues, but there is nothing it can do about the (real's) overall trend," said Drausio Giacomelli, head of emerging market strategy at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The problem is purely fiscal, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot solve the fiscal problem," he said.\par \par \uc2 The real is down 8.2% against the dollar this year. Aside from the Libyan dinar and Sudanese pound, which both suffered massive one-off devaluations, it is the world's worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar this year, according to Refinitiv data.\par \par \uc2 The government is expected to extend emergency aid to millions of poor people as the second wave of the COVID-19 pandemic sweeps the country. But it is unclear whether it will be accompanied by corresponding spending cuts over the longer term.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action on Tuesday followed two similar \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  each on Thursday and Friday, the first time this year it had sold dollars in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Last year it sold almost $25 billion in the spot market as the real depreciated some 30% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever; Editing by Andrew Heavens, Jonathan Oatis and Richard Chang) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-2T21:31:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210302eh3201z2e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
