\par\par \b \uc2 Moody`s rates B2(hyb) Banrisul`s proposed Tier 2 notes\b0\par\par\uc2 3,024 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2021\par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2021 \par \par \uc2 New York, January 15, 2021 -- \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  today assigned a B2(hyb) long term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subordinated debt rating to \uc2 Banco do Estado do Rio Grande do Sul S.A\uc2 .'s (\uc2 Banrisul\uc2 ) proposed contractual non-viability Tier 2 subordinated notes issuance. The notes will be due in 2031 and callable after five years.\par \par \uc2 The capital securities are Basel III-compliant, and their terms and conditions have been defined so as to qualify the notes for treatment as Tier 2 capital pursuant to Brazilian regulation.\par \par \uc2 The following rating was assigned to \uc2 Banco do Estado do Rio Grande do Sul S.A\uc2 .:\par \par \uc2 - Long term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subordinate debt rating of B2(hyb)\par \par \uc2 RATINGS RATIONALE\par \par \uc2 The B2(hyb) rating assigned to \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's proposed Tier 2 subordinated notes is positioned two notches below \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's ba3 adjusted baseline credit assessment (adjusted BCA), in line with \uc2 Moody's\uc2  standard notching guidance for contractual non-viability subordinated debt with full or partial principal write-down triggered at or close to the point of non-viability.\par \par \uc2 The B2(hyb) assigned to the proposed Tier 2 notes reflects the risk of a full or partial write-down of principal in the event that (i) \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's common equity Tier 1 ratio falls below 4.5%, (ii) the bank receives a capital injection from government funds, (iii) the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2  makes a discretionary determination that a write-down is necessary, or (iv) the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bank or establishes a special administrative regime at \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's ba3 baseline credit assessment (BCA) reflects its challenged asset risk and profitability, adequate capitalization as well as its funding structure predominantly made up of low-cost core deposits. The BCA also incorporates \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's strong regional franchise whereby a relevant share of its deposits and \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are based within the state of Rio Grande do Sul (unrated) which, however, continues to be under significant fiscal distress.\par \par \uc2 In terms of asset quality, \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's loan book is predominantly focused on low-risk payroll loans to state and federal employees, which accounted for 46% of total loans as of September 2020. Another 30% are loans to small and mid-sized enterprises and unsecured consumer loans. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  notes that the bank restructured and renegotiated loan installments of BRL 745 million, approximately 17% of its interest income from lending in the first nine months of 2020. As a result of the coronavirus pandemic. As the grace periods on these renegotiations end, the bank could face latent delinquency pressures. \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's problem loan ratio as calculated by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  in the third quarter of 2020 was 3.89%, up from 2.66% in 2019. The bank's loan loss reserves were 7.76% of total loans, providing a buffer against asset risk given that they cover problem loans by almost 200%, as calculated by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The challenges \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 ' faces in recovering its profitability in 2021 to historical levels are also reflected in its BCA. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  notes that constrained business volumes as Brazil posts only a moderate economic recovery following 2020's contraction, higher loan loss provisions from asset risk pressures and the low interest rate environment will continue to strain margins and the bank's profitability. \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's net income to tangible assets was 0.74% as of September 2020, down from 1.3% in 2019, driven by these factors as well as the effects of the rate cap on overdrafts, which lowered its net interest margin. In addition, because \uc2 Banrisul\uc2  is a state-owned bank, it has limited flexibility in reducing operating costs to counter margin pressures or to invest in products and technology, in line with that of its private bank peers.\par \par \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's capitalization, as measured by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  preferred ratio of tangible common equity relative to risk weighted assets, remains adequate at 8.6% as of September 2020, in line with 2019 levels. Following the issuance of the Tier 2 notes, the bank's regulatory capital will rise to around 20%, from 16.2% as of September 2020.\par \par \uc2 With over 50% of the bank's tangible assets currently invested in sovereign government bonds, \uc2 Banrisul\uc2  has a strong liquidity buffer, in addition to limited reliance on market funding, at modest 13% of its tangible assets. However, these investments are now yielding a lot less in Brazil's low rate environment, also negative for the bank's profitability.\par \par \uc2 FACTORS THAT COULD LEAD TO AN UPGRADE OR DOWNGRADE OF THE RATING\par \par \uc2 The ratings of the Tier 2 notes are notched from \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's adjusted BCA. As such, the ratings of the securities will move in tandem with \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's adjusted BCA, which does not incorporate affiliate support and is at the same level as the BCA. \uc2 Banrisul\uc2 's BCA could be upgraded if its asset quality exhibits significant improvement, combined with a sustained rise in its recurring profitability and capitalization.\par \par \uc2 A downgrade in the BCA could result from greater than expected deterioration in asset risk and profitability, or further declines in adjusted capitalization.\par \par \uc2 The principal methodology used in this rating was Banks Methodology published in November 2019 and available at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.moodys.com/researchdocumentcontentpage.aspx?docid=PBC_1147865"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.moodys.com/researchdocumentcontentpage.aspx?docid=PBC_1147865}}}\uc2 . Alternatively, please see the Rating Methodologies page on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.moodys.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.moodys.com}}}\uc2  for a copy of this methodology.\par \par \uc2 REGULATORY DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 For further specification of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  key rating assumptions and sensitivity analysis, see the sections Methodology Assumptions and Sensitivity to Assumptions in the disclosure form. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Rating Symbols and Definitions can be found at: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.moodys.com/researchdocumentcontentpage.aspx?docid=PBC_79004"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.moodys.com/researchdocumentcontentpage.aspx?docid=PBC_79004}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For ratings issued on a program, series, category/class of debt or security this announcement provides certain regulatory disclosures in relation to each rating of a subsequently issued bond or note of the same series, category/class of debt, security or pursuant to a program for which the ratings are derived exclusively from existing ratings in accordance with \uc2 Moody's\uc2  rating practices. For ratings issued on a support provider, this announcement provides certain regulatory disclosures in relation to the credit rating action on the support provider and in relation to each particular credit rating action for securities that derive their credit ratings from the support provider's credit rating. For provisional ratings, this announcement provides certain regulatory disclosures in relation to the provisional rating assigned, and in relation to a definitive rating that may be assigned subsequent to the final issuance of the d