\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil's real jumps, stocks hit record on U.S. stimulus hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2020\par 16:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Shashank Nayar\par     Dec 29 (Reuters) - Brazil's real jumped on Tuesday and stock\par markets hit an all-time high as upbeat data, hopes of a big U.S.\par stimulus package and COVID-19 vaccination campaigns across major\par countries lifted expectations of a speedy global recovery.\par     The real bounced off a one-month low to trade up 0.5%\par at 5.2136, as a weaker dollar supported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par region. \par     Data showed Brazil's jobless rate fell for the first time\par this year in the three months through October, while a survey\par showed consumer confidence increased in December for the first\par time in three months.\par     "The wider fiscal package from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Congress\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the\par Brazilian economy getting better has helped improve the investor\par outlook towards Brazilian risk assets," said Mauricio Une,\par senior economist at Rabobank.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on Monday for the first time in two months after\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slipped to a one-month low against the dollar.\par \par     Sao Paulo stocks hit a record high, tracking global\par stocks on hopes that a $2.3 trillion stimulus package signed\par into law by President Trump on Sunday will be approved by the\par Senate.\par     The package covers $1.4 trillion in spending to fund\par government agencies and $892 billion in COVID-19 relief,\par including $2,000 relief cheques to help cushion the economic\par impact of the pandemic.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of oil exporters Mexico and Colombia\par   gained as oil prices rallied on hopes that an\par expanding U.S. pandemic aid would spur fuel demand. \par     The world's top copper producer Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par gained on higher copper prices, which rose for a fourth straight\par session.\par     MSCI's index of Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rose 1.3%, the most in nearly three weeks, while stocks\par  gained nearly 1%. \par \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :\par     \par                                        Latest       Daily %\par                                                      change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets                    1262.42        0.81\par  MSCI LatAm                               2441.86        1.02\par  Brazil Bovespa                         118906.97       -0.18\par  Mexico IPC                              44232.23        0.79\par  Chile IPSA                               4188.99        0.21\par  Argentina MerVal                        51163.93       1.347\par  Colombia COLCAP                          1431.58        0.04\par                                                    \par                                                              \par            \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                   Latest       Daily %\par                                                      change\par  Brazil real                               5.2136        0.48\par  Mexico peso                              19.9979        0.02\par  Chile peso                                 711.5      