\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Brazil cenbank weighing up  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action to prevent 'overhedge' volatility - director\b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2020\par 19:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Jamie McGeever and Marcela Ayres\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Nov 6 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market before the end of the year to minimize any potential disruption from large anticipated outflows related to banks' hedging activities, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in a live online forum hosted by Itau Macrovision, economic policy director Fabio Kanczuk said officials were "all over" the issue, in which banks must unwind their so-called "overhedge" positions on overseas equity investments.\par \par \uc2 Unwinding these positions before the end of the year is expected to prompt a hefty outflow. One trader at a large bank in Sao Paulo put the potential flow at $15-$17 billion.\par \par \uc2 "The market needs to be big, big enough to withstand a very large (out)flow at the end of the year, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thinking about alternatives on how not to let this flow be disruptive," Kanczuk said.\par \par \uc2 "We are not sure the market will be able to absorb this, and think we will need to help," he added.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real has been one of the world's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, losing almost 30% of its value against the dollar since January.\par \par \uc2 Kanczuk said that had been down to a steady decline in official interest rates to the current record low 2.00%, but more recently due to growing concerns over Brazil's fiscal health.\par \par \uc2 He said Brazil's fiscal situation had not become de-anchored yet, and nor had inflation expectations, meaning the bank's forward guidance pledge to keep interest rates low for a long time remained in place. Kanczuk adding he saw no reason to provide extra stimulus.\par \par \uc2 On inflation, he struck a sanguine tone. The recent spike fueled by food prices has been higher than expected and is likely to continue for a few more months, but inflation will not be a problem next year or 2022, he said.\par \par \uc2 The recent spike is temporary and should not affect policy. Policymakers are watching inflation developments but remain "super calm", he said.\par \par \uc2 Figures on Friday showed that monthly inflation in October jumped to 0.86%, the highest for any October since 2002, while the annual rate rose to 3.90%. (Reporting by Marcela Ayres and Jamie McGeever; Editing by Alison Williams) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-11-6T19:48:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201106egb6037l6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
