\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Sao Paulo falls 4.25% for new restrictions in Europe over pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Sao Paulo, 28 Oct (EFE).- The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange fell by 4.25% on Wednesday and its Ibovespa index, a parquet benchmark, closed with 95,368 points, hit by new restrictions in Europe to contain the second wave of covid-19. The Paulista corro chained its fourth consecutive fall, while the US dollar continues to climb and today appreciated 1.31% to close quoted at 5.75 reais for buying and selling. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached 5.79 reais during the day, the highest value since mid-May, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inject more than $1 billion into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange followed in the light of the rest of the international squares, which experienced sessions dyed red by uncertainty around the consequences and magnitude of the second wave of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2  The two largest economies in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (\uc2 EU\uc2 ), France and Germany, announced new social estating measures on Wednesday, including the closure of bars and restaurants and the paralysis of other non-essential activities. Investors are concerned about the drift of the pandemic and fear that the economic recovery process will not be as fast as thought. Added to this fear of the second wave of covid-19 in Europe is the uncertainty of fiscal drift in Brazil. Economic operators appreciate that the reform agenda, including an administrative one, and fiscal adjustment of the Government does not advance in Congress, something they consider essential to rebalancing the country's weakened public accounts. The nominal government deficit reached the equivalent of 16.70% of Brazil's Gross Domestic Product (GDP) in the annual cumulative to August, compared to 5.89% in the same period in 2019, while gross debt has soared to 88.8% of GDP. However, the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange lost 4,236 units to its cumulative score. Turnover reached 29,339 million reais (about $5.1 billion) in a total of $3,975,643 financial transactions, according to preliminary data at the end of the session. Virtually no stock value ended the day in positive and among the biggest losses was the air sector with 9.58% and 9.03% collapses in the shares of the airlines Azul and Gol, respectively. The most negotiated titles of the session were the regulars of retail manager Via Varejo (-7.36%) and the preferred petrobras state oil company (-6.09%) \uc2 Bradesco Bank\uc2  (-5.66%).\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201028egas0085j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
