\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  drop against strong dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2020\par 16:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Susan Mathew\par     Aug 21 (Reuters) - Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened on\par Friday, as the dollar surged on indications of stalling global\par economic recovery from a pandemic induced slump. \par     Brazil's real fell more than 1%, staying near\par three-month lows, while sliding oil prices weighed on crude\par exporter Colombia's peso.\par     Mexico's peso swung either side of flat as the dollar\par surged after disappointing business activity data from Europe\par sank the euro. The data comes on the heels of a U.S. report\par showing that jobless claims rose last week, signaling a nascent\par rebound in global growth may be stalling.\par     In Brazil, the lower house of Congress on Thursday voted to\par uphold a presidential veto blocking pay increases for civil\par servants, a victory for President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jair Bolsonaro\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Economy\par Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Paulo Guedes\f28 \uc2 \f2  in their drive to maintain fiscal\par discipline.\par     The real had dived earlier on Thursday after the Senate\par overturned the veto. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is on course to end the week\par about 3% lower. \par     The move "shows that the resistance to implement an austere\par fiscal (policy) is bigger than previously expected," said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding they expect fiscal discussions\par to remain in the spotlight as the government is to deliver the\par 2021 budget law by the end of August.\par     "We anticipate (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue\par softening moves with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in swaps and spot if the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bleeds heavily."   \par     An unabated rise in COVID-19 cases also weighed. The number\par of reported COVID-19 deaths in Latin America passed 250,000 on\par Thursday, as the virus afflicts the region, which has become the\par worst hit in the world.\par     Stocks in Sao Paulo fell on a financials and\par material drag. Education firm Cogna slid to the\par bottom on the Bovespa after it reported a loss for the second\par quarter. \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1347 GMT:\par   Stock indexes           Latest   Daily %\par                                    change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets    1087.45     0.64\par                                    \par  MSCI LatAm               1946.76    -0.04\par                                    \par  Brazil Bovespa         100962.68     -0.5\par                                    \par  Mexico IPC                     -        -\par                                    \par  Chile IPSA               3975.79    -0.05\par                                    \par  Argentina MerVal               -        -\par                                    \par  Colombia COLCAP          1170.77    -0.29\par                                    \par                                           \par       \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2           Latest   Daily %\par                                    change\par  Brazil real               5.6163    -1.13\par                                    \par  Mexico peso              22.0850    -0.05\par                                    \par  Chile 