\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Most Latam stocks track Wall St higher after Fed minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2020\par 21:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Updates prices)\par     By Susan Mathew and Ambar Warrick\par     April 8 (Reuters) - Latin American stocks extended gains to\par a third session on Wednesday, after Wall Street strengthened\par gains on the release of minutes of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par last meeting, while regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also firmed against a\par stronger dollar.\par     The minutes reaffirmed the Fed's resolve to take any\par measures needed to stabilize the economy in the face the\par coronavirus pandemic, panic over which has wiped trillions off\par the global indices. \par     "It seems as if the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee was\par pretty much in unison behind the broader decisions they've\par made," said Kevin Flanagan, head of fixed income strategy at\par Wisdomtree Asset Management, New York.\par     As U.S. stocks gained, Latam bourses followed. Brazil's\par Bovespa climbed 3.5%, while most other regional stock\par indexes rose between 1% and 3.7%. Mexico's \f28 \uc2 \f2 IPC\f28 \uc2 \f2  index,\par however, dipped slightly in its last trading day for the week.\par     Financial markets in Mexico, Argentina, Colombia will resume\par trading on Monday after an extended eater weekend. \par     The virus' death toll and spread continued to increase,\par while its economic impact loomed over markets as more countries\par impose lockdowns in a bid to stem its spread. As of Tuesday,\par Mexico registered 2,785 cases of coronavirus infection, and a\par death toll of 141. \par     Emerging market assets were hit particularly hard by the\par drop in risk appetite, with the MSCI's Latin American stocks\par  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  indexes now\par trading just above multi-year lows.\par     Brazil's real erased early losses to trade 1.4%\par firmer, as did Mexico's peso.\par     Anticipation ahead of a key OPEC+ meeting on Thursday, which\par is expected to result in major oil producers cutting production,\par also kept markets on their toes. \par     Oil and gas heavyweight \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the biggest\par boost to Brazil's Bovespa, adding more than 2.5% after it\par outlined further cost cuts. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Roberto Campos Neto\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par pledged aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to\par prop up the real, which has lost nearly a third of its value\par over the last quarter.\par     In Chile, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose 1.6%, while stocks\par  added 1.9%. Data on Wednesday showed inflation in the\par 12 months to March rose 3.7% in the country, but was within the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range.\par         \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 1910 GMT:\par   Stock indexes           Latest   Daily %\par                                    change\par  MSCI Emerging Markets     875.42    -0.31\par                                    \par  MSCI LatAm               1677.12     3.56\par                                    \par  Brazil Bovespa          78988.88     3.45\par                                    \par  Mexico IPC              34403.91    -0.35\par                                    \par  Chile IPSA           