\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Brazil lawmakers pass 'war budget' as coronavirus cases top 10,000\b0\par\par\uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2020\par 02:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (New throughout, adds comments from president of \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Roberto Campos)\par \par \uc2 By Gabriela Mello\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, April 4 (Reuters) - Brazil's lower house of Congress approved a constitutional amendment for a "war budget" to separate coronavirus-related spending from the government's main budget and shield the economy as the country surpassed 10,000 confirmed cases.\par \par \uc2 The war budget still needs the Senate's approval by three-fifths of the votes in two rounds expected to take place next week.\par \par \uc2 Late on Friday, the lower house approved the main text of the bill with 423 votes in favor and one opposed in a second round of voting after a first score of 505 in favor and two against.\par \par \uc2 The amendment creates an extraordinary regime to prevent expenses related to a "state of emergency" decree triggered by the pandemic, which is valid until Dec. 31, from being mixed with the federal budget over the same period.\par \par \uc2 Besides easing fiscal and budgetary constraints to speed up measures tackling the outbreak, the war budget also grants the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  emergency bond-buying powers to stabilize financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "We must ensure money reaches key sectors, so as of this week we'll start discussing what we want to buy and what kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we want to make," \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, Roberto Campos, said in a live presentation to XP brokerage on Saturday evening.\par \par \uc2 Campos sees the ongoing crisis as much worse than the 2008 one, noting that the COVID-19 outbreak has unleashed the largest outflow ever seen in emerging markets, with the Brazilian stock exchange and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the most affected.\par \par \uc2 "But we moved quickly to boost market liquidity and we still have an arsenal of measures to adopt," he said, adding the financial system is solid enough and actions altogether have the potential to inject up to 1.2 trillion reais ($224.32 billion) in liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Campos declined to comment on the duration of social isolation measures, but urged market participants to avoid breaching contracts, echoing earlier remarks by Economy Minister \uc2 Paulo Guedes\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We can renegotiate everything, rents, salaries, but we must not default," Guedes told representatives of retail and services industries separately on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 He recognized, however, that some measures taken to "oxygenate the economy," such as a cut in banks' reserve requirements, are not yet having the desired effect. "Banks are conservative and the credit is not reaching those who need it," he said.\par \par \uc2 Guedes reiterated that, despite the focus on emergency measures, he believes economic recovery still relies on structural reforms including tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 "Our way out of this crisis further ahead will be through tax cuts ... Creating jobs must be easy, cheap and stimulating," he said.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 Brazil's coronavirus death toll rose to 431 from 359, while the number of confirmed cases jumped to 10,278 from 9,056, according to Health Ministry figures released on Saturday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is among a number of countries struggling to get medical supplies from China.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Saturday, President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  asked Indian Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  for support in supplying pharmaceutical inputs for the production of hydroxychloroquine.\par \par \uc2 "Had a productive telephone conversation with President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  about how India and Brazil can join forces against the COVID-19 pandemic," Modi wrote on his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 Bolsonaro's approval rating has fallen to its lowest level since he took office last year amid mounting criticism of his handling of the public health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Despite downplaying COVID-19 as a "little flu" multiple times, Bolsonaro called his supporters for a national day of fasting and prayer on Sunday to "free Brazil from this evil" epidemic.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 5.3495 reais) (Reporting by Gabriela Mello; Editing by David Clarke, Alistair Bell, Richard Chang and David Gregorio) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-4-5T01:52:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200404eg4400pm9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
